    "q-214406": {
      "dom_id": "step-q214406",
      "name": "Question 7",
      "problem": "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A=\\{∗,⋆,∘\\}$ defined as \n\n$$\nR=\\{(∗,∗),(⋆,⋆),(∘,∘)\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $R$ is reflexive\n\nII. $R$ is symmetric\n\nIII. $R$ is transitive\n\nIV. $R$ is an equivalence relation",
      "worked_solution": "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is reflexive because $(∗,∗),(⋆,⋆),(∘,∘)\\in R.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric. In our relation, there are no pairs $(x,y)$ where $x\\ne y,$ so symmetry is automatically satisfied.\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in \\{∗,⋆,∘\\},$ if  $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,z)\\in R,$  then $(x,z)\\in R.$ It's usually sufficient to check only the cases when both pairs have different components. However, transitivity is vacuously true in this case since the relation contains no pairs with distinct components.\n\n- Since $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, it is an equivalence relation.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
