    "q-214278": {
      "dom_id": "step-q214278",
      "name": "Question 10",
      "problem": "Consider the relation $R$ on the set $A=\\{m,n,p,q,r\\}$ defined as \n\n$$\nR=\\{(m,m),(m,p),(p,m),(p,p),(q,q),(r,r)\\}.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $R$ is reflexive\n\nII. $R$ is symmetric\n\nIII. $R$ is transitive\n\nIV. $R$ is an equivalence relation",
      "worked_solution": "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nLet's consider each of the properties in turn.\n\n- The relation $R$ is *not* reflexive because $(n,n)\\notin R.$\n\n- The relation $R$ is symmetric because $(y,x)\\in R$ whenever $(x,y)\\in R.$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation $R$ is transitive because for all $x,y,z\\in \\{m,n,p,q,r\\},$ if $(x,y)\\in R$ and $(y,z)\\in R,$ then $(x,z)\\in R.$ Recall that it's sufficient to check only the cases when both pairs have different components: \n\n- $(m,p)\\in R$ and $(p,m)\\in R,$ and also $(m,m)\\in R$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- $(p,m)\\in R$ and $(m,p)\\in R,$ and also $(p,p)\\in R$ $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- The relation is *not* an equivalence relation since it's not reflexive.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\"",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
