    "q-214244": {
      "dom_id": "step-q214244",
      "name": "Question 12",
      "problem": "Which of the following relations are equivalence relations on $P(Z)?$\n\nI. $A\\,R\\,B$ if $A\\cap B=∅$\n\nII. $A\\,R\\,B$ if $A\\cap \\{1\\}=B\\cap \\{1\\}$\n\nIII. $A\\,R\\,B$ if $A\\cup B⊆A$",
      "worked_solution": "Given a relation $R$ on a set $A$:\n\n- $R$ is reflexive if and only if for all $x\\in A,$ $x\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is symmetric if and only if for all $x,y\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y,$ then $y\\,R\\,x.$\n\n- $R$ is transitive if and only if for all $x,y,z\\in A,$ if $x\\,R\\,y$ and $y\\,R\\,z,$ then $x\\,R\\,z.$\n\nIf $R$ is reflexive, symmetric, and transitive, then $R$ is an equivalence relation.\n\nWith this in mind, let's consider each of the given relations.\n\n- Relation I is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not reflexive because $\\{0\\}\\cap \\{0\\}\\ne ∅.$\n\n- Relation II is an equivalence relation:\n\n- It is reflexive because for all $A\\in P(Z),$ we have $A\\cap \\{1\\}=A\\cap \\{1\\}.$\n\n- It is symmetric because for all $A,B\\in P(Z),$ if $A\\cap \\{1\\}=B\\cap \\{1\\},$ then $B\\cap \\{1\\}=A\\cap \\{1\\}.$\n\n- It is transitive because for all $A,B,C\\in P(Z),$ if $A\\cap \\{1\\}=B\\cap \\{1\\}$ and $B\\cap \\{1\\}=C\\cap \\{1\\},$ then $A\\cap \\{1\\}=C\\cap \\{1\\}.$\n\n- Relation III is not an equivalence relation. For example, it is not symmetric because $\\{0,1\\}\\cup \\{0\\}⊆\\{0,1\\}$ but $\\{0\\}\\cup \\{0,1\\}⊈\\{0\\}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only\".",
